---
description: Never leave processes the agent started running after the turn (API, Gateway, docker, watchers)
alwaysApply: true
---

# No orphaned processes

Do not start a long-running process and leave it on. If you started it, you stop it before the turn ends — including when the task fails, you switch approach, or you are only “checking that it boots.”

## What counts

Hosts and daemons: `Lyo.TestApi`, `Lyo.TestGateway`, `dotnet run` / `dotnet watch`, `python3 scripts/docker/run.py` / `run_workbench.py up`, `docker compose up`, Redis/Postgres you started ad hoc, file watchers, k6 servers, anything backgrounded with `block_until_ms: 0` that does not exit on its own.

Finite commands are fine (`dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, one-shot scripts). Await them; do not background them unless you will reap them in the same turn.

## Required cleanup

- Stop the process (Ctrl+C equivalent / kill the pid from the terminal header / `run_workbench.py down`).
- Confirm it is gone (`pgrep`, closed terminal footer with `exit_code`) before finishing.
- Do not hand the user a running stack you launched unless they **explicitly** asked to leave it up.

```text
# ❌ BAD — start the API and end the turn
dotnet run --project Lyo.Net/Tools/Lyo.TestApi
# (leave it listening)

# ✅ GOOD — boot only as long as you need, then stop
dotnet run --project Lyo.Net/Tools/Lyo.TestApi
# …curl / exercise …
# kill the process; verify nothing is still bound to the port
```
